From: vyos-ls1046a-build CI <ci@local>
Date: Tue, 01 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fman: port: fix fman_port_active_list double-insertion for O/H ports (phase 1c/1d fix)

Board-found 2026-09-01 while diagnosing an unrelated production VLAN
offload bug report on .185: a plain routed (non-VLAN) TCP flow through
a completely unrelated physical port (eth3, hw port 0x10) failed
hardware offload with "REPLACE rollback fe_flow_insert=-19" (ENODEV)
on every single attempt, forever. Traced to
fman_cc_tree_install()'s (dpaa_fman_caps.c) call to
fman_port_lookup_rx(fm, 0x10), which returned NULL for a port that has
been working since well before this session.

Root cause: 0177/0178 register an O/H port into fman_port_active_list
at PROBE time (fman_port_probe()) so a not-yet-brought-up O/H port is
discoverable by the "bringup" debugfs tool. But the ORIGINAL 0115
registration point, at the end of fman_port_init(), unconditionally
does its own list_add_tail() on the same port->node -- and this
session's Phase 1c/1d board testing did call "bringup 4" (dmesg:
"oh_test bringup 0x04 OK" at boot+140s), which drives
fman_port_config()+fman_port_init() for that same, already-probe-time-
registered O/H port object. That second list_add_tail() on an already-
linked struct list_head splices the node into the list a second time
without unlinking it from its first position, corrupting the shared
doubly-linked list for every port on the fman -- not just the O/H
port. fman_port_lookup_rx()'s list_for_each_entry walk over the
corrupted list then silently fails to reach unrelated RX ports
(eth3/0x10 in this case), and every caller (fman_cc_tree_install(),
i.e. every port's VLAN CC-tree AND plain ehash-vs-CC-tree dispatch)
gets -ENODEV. No crash, no WARN -- list.h's plain (non-DEBUG_LIST)
list_add_tail() has no double-add detection, so this was a silent,
board-wide production regression from a debug-only test tool call.

0175's probe-time registration comment claimed this was "safe" because
port->node is kzalloc'd/zeroed and list_add_tail() "only writes
through it, never reads it as an existing link first" -- true for the
FIRST call, but that reasoning never accounted for fman_port_init()
making a SECOND, unconditional call on the same node later. It also
can't be fixed with list_empty(&port->node) as the guard: kzalloc
leaves ->next/->prev NULL rather than self-pointing, since port->node
is never INIT_LIST_HEAD'd, so list_empty() is not well-defined for a
never-linked node here.

Fix: add struct fman_port::active_list_added (kzalloc-zeroed, so
false until set), checked and set under fman_port_active_lock at BOTH
call sites (fman_port_init()'s original 0115 site, and 0175/0178's
probe-time site). The node is now linked into fman_port_active_list at
most once regardless of call order -- probe-then-init (O/H ports) or
init-only (RX/TX ports, unaffected: active_list_added starts false, so
their existing single list_add_tail() still fires exactly as before).

Board-tested 2026-09-01: this exact double-registration state (from
the "bringup 4" call earlier in .185's current uptime) is why the live
VLAN offload investigation this session was giving inconsistent
results -- it was chasing a live-corrupted port registry, not a VLAN-
specific defect. A reboot is required to clear the already-corrupted
in-memory list on any board that already ran "bringup" before this
fix; this patch only prevents the corruption going forward.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate | Risk-Tier: A (adds one bool field + a
guard around two pre-existing list_add_tail() call sites; RX/TX ports
take the exact same single-add path as before, byte-identical
behavior for every port type except O/H, where it now prevents a
double-add instead of allowing one)
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
index 6cabfcb4dad4..1dea6a057aec 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
@@ -491,6 +491,7 @@ struct fman_port_dts_params {
 struct fman_port {
 	void *fm;
 	struct list_head node;		/* fman_port_active_list (0115) */
+	bool active_list_added;	/* guards list_add_tail() idempotency (0181) */
 	u32 ctrl_params_page;	/* FM_CTL params page MURAM off (0116) */
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct fman_rev_info rev_info;
@@ -2187,7 +2188,10 @@ int fman_port_init(struct fman_port *port)
 	port->cfg = NULL;

 	mutex_lock(&fman_port_active_lock);
-	list_add_tail(&port->node, &fman_port_active_list);
+	if (!port->active_list_added) {
+		list_add_tail(&port->node, &fman_port_active_list);
+		port->active_list_added = true;
+	}
 	mutex_unlock(&fman_port_active_lock);

 	return 0;
@@ -2624,13 +2628,29 @@ static int fman_port_probe(struct platform_device *of_dev)
 	 * consumer -- nothing else will ever call config()/init() for one,
 	 * a debugfs/production caller has to find it FIRST in order to do
 	 * so. Register here at probe time instead, so a probed-but-not-yet-
-	 * brought-up O/H port is still discoverable. Safe: port->node is
-	 * kzalloc'd (zeroed), and list_add_tail() only writes through it,
-	 * never reads it as an existing link first.
+	 * brought-up O/H port is still discoverable.
+	 *
+	 * 0181 fix: a "bringup" debugfs call later drives this SAME port
+	 * object through fman_port_config()+fman_port_init(), and
+	 * fman_port_init() unconditionally did its own list_add_tail() (the
+	 * original 0115 RX/TX registration point) with no check for an
+	 * already-linked node. Since port->node is never INIT_LIST_HEAD'd
+	 * (kzalloc leaves ->next/->prev NULL, so list_empty() can't be used
+	 * as the guard), a second list_add_tail() on the same node spliced
+	 * it into fman_port_active_list a second time without unlinking the
+	 * first, corrupting the shared list for EVERY port on the fman
+	 * (silently breaks fman_port_lookup_rx() for real RX ports, e.g.
+	 * ENODEV from fman_cc_tree_install() on a completely unrelated
+	 * physical port -- board-found 2026-09-01 chasing an unrelated VLAN
+	 * offload bug report). port->active_list_added now guards both call
+	 * sites so the node is linked at most once regardless of order.
 	 */
 	if (port_type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH) {
 		mutex_lock(&fman_port_active_lock);
-		list_add_tail(&port->node, &fman_port_active_list);
+		if (!port->active_list_added) {
+			list_add_tail(&port->node, &fman_port_active_list);
+			port->active_list_added = true;
+		}
 		mutex_unlock(&fman_port_active_lock);
 	}

